<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Pashalik of Vidin</h1><p class="st-meta">1794 CE – 1807 CE (approx.) · capital Vidin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rebel domain of Osman Pazvantoğlu on the Danube, who defied the Ottoman sultan and let loose raiders across Bulgaria and Wallachia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-pazvantoglu">Osman Pazvantoğlu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III's</a> military reforms and seized control of Vidin around 1794. He gathered disbanded janissaries and kardzhali bandits, and in 1798 defeated a large Ottoman army sent to besiege the town. Unable to remove him, the sultan pardoned him and appointed him pasha of Vidin.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-pazvantoglu">Pazvantoğlu</a> ruled as a warlord with his own army and treasury, and conducted his own diplomacy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His bands raided much of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and crossed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a>, burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> in 1800. He backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissary</a> dahije who seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjak-of-smederevo">Pashalik of Belgrade</a>, whose abuses helped provoke the Serbian uprising of 1804.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-pazvantoglu">Pazvantoğlu</a> died in 1807, and his successors soon returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> to Ottoman control. His career showed the weakness of central authority under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a>; Bulgarian memory recalls the kardzhali years as a time of terror.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pashalik-of-vidin.jpg" alt="Pashalik of Vidin" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Klearchos Kapoutsis · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baba_Vida_Klearchos_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1794 CE – 1807 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vidin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Osman Pazvantoğlu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ottoman Turkish, Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pazvantoğlu's Vidin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=pashalik-of-vidin" aria-label="Pashalik of Vidin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5228 620 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pashalik of Vidin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5548 876L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vidin. <a href="/atlas?year=1800&amp;focus=pashalik-of-vidin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
